REGIONAL REGULATION OF TANAH BUMBU REGENCY NUMBER 12 YEAR 2024
CONCERNING
IMPLEMENTATION OF WOMEN'S EMPOWERMENT AND
CHILD PROTECTION
BY THE GRACE OF ALMIGHTY GOD
THE REGENT OF TANAH BUMBU,

Considering : a. that Women and Children have the right to obtain protection from torture, threats, pressure, and have the right to obtain equal treatment and opportunities to obtain justice and welfare in accordance with the mandate of the 1945 Constitution of the Republic of Indonesia;
b. that Women's Empowerment and protecting the rights of Children are obligations of the Regional Government and Society in the Region;
c. that based on the provisions of Article 12 paragraph (2) letter b and letter H Division of Government Affairs in the Field of Women's Empowerment and Child Protection Appendix to Law Number 23 Year 2014 concerning Regional Government as has been amended several times most recently by Law Number 6 Year 2023 concerning Stipulation of Government Regulation in Lieu of Law Number 2 Year 2022 concerning Job Creation into Law, the Regional Government has the authority to implement Women's Empowerment and Child Protection in the Region;
d. that based on the considerations as referred to in letter a, letter b, and letter c, it is necessary to establish a Regional Regulation concerning Women's Empowerment and Child Protection;

In View of : 1. Law Number 4 year 1979 concerning Child Welfare (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 1979 Number 32, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3143);
2. Law Number 7 Year 1984 concerning Ratification of the Convention on the Elimination of All Forms of Discrimination Against Women (Convention on the Elemination of All Forms of Discrimination Againts Women) (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 1984 Number 29, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3277);
3. Law Number 39 Year 1999 concerning Human Rights (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 1999 Number 165, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3886);
4. Law Number 23 Year 2002 concerning Child Protection (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2002 Number 102, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4235) as has been amended several times most recently by Law Number 1 Year 2023 concerning the Criminal Code (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2023 Number 1, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 6842);
5. Law Number 13 Year 2003 concerning Manpower (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2003 Number 39, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4279) as has been amended several times most recently by Law Number 6 Year 2023 concerning Stipulation of Government Regulation in Lieu of Law Number 2 Year 2022 concerning Job Creation into Law (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2023 Number 41, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 6856);
6. Law Number 6 Year 2014 concerning Villages (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2014 Number 7, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5495) as has been amended several times most recently by Law Number 6 Year 2023 concerning Stipulation of Government Regulation in Lieu of Law Number 2 Year 2022 concerning Job Creation into Law (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2023 Number 41, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 6856);
7. Law Number 23 Year 2014 concerning Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2014 Number 244, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5587) as has been amended several times most recently by Law Number 6 Year 2023 concerning Stipulation of Government Regulation in Lieu of Law Number 2 Year 2022 concerning Job Creation into Law (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2023 Number 41, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 6856);
8. Law Number 8 Year 2016 concerning Persons with Disabilities (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2016 Number 69, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5871);
9. Law Number 17 Year 2023 concerning Health (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2023 Number 105, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 6887);
10. Presidential Regulation Number 25 Year 2021 concerning Child-Friendly Regency/City Policy (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2021 Number 96);
11. Regional Regulation of Tanah Bumbu Regency Number 19 Year 2016 concerning Establishment and Composition of Regional Apparatus (Regional Gazette of Tanah Bumbu Regency Year 2016 Number 19) as has been amended several times most recently by Regional Regulation of Tanah Bumbu Regency Number 5 Year 2021 concerning Second Amendment to Regional Regulation of Tanah Bumbu Regency Number 19 Year 2016 concerning Establishment and Composition of Regional Apparatus (Regional Gazette of Tanah Bumbu Regency Year 2021 Number 5);
12. Regional Regulation of Tanah Bumbu Regency Number 11 Year 2021 concerning Implementation of Child-Friendly Regency (Regional Gazette of Tanah Bumbu Regency Year 2021 Number 11, Supplement to the Regional Gazette of Tanah Bumbu Regency Number 126);

Stipulates : REGIONAL REGULATION With Mutual Approval

Part Two Protection of Women's Rights
Article 13
(1) The Regional Government guarantees protection of women's rights.
(2) Women's rights as referred to in paragraph (1) include the fields of:
a. education;
b. health;
c. employment;
d. politics;
e. law; and
f. economy, culture, and social.

Article 34
(1) Protection of School-Age Children as referred to in Article 22 paragraph (1) letter c includes:
a. receiving attention and guidance from teachers and/or supervisors without discrimination;
b. receiving attention and affection from family;
c. receiving religious guidance at school and in the Community environment;
d. receiving prevention services, health rehabilitation and care;
e. receiving compulsory education for a minimum of 9 (nine) years and being able to complete senior secondary school supported by a friendly and conducive environment;
f. receiving education guarantees;
g. receiving guidance and counseling; and
h. being provided with adequate places for play and sports.
(2) Every person and/or any party is obligated to protect School-Age Children from acts of violence, sexual harassment and food containing hazardous substances in the family, school, Office/agency/institution and Community environment.
(3) Every person and/or any party is obligated to protect School-Age Children from actions to suspend and/or expel Children from school and/or cause Children to drop out of school.

CHAPTER X COOPERATION
Article 49
(1) In the effort to implement Women's Empowerment and Child Protection, the Regional Government may cooperate.
(2) Cooperation as referred to in paragraph (1) is conducted with:
a. government;
b. provincial government;
c. other Regional Governments;
d. vertical agencies;
e. advocates;
f. social workers;
g. companion volunteers;
h. spiritual counselors; and
i. non-governmental organizations engaged in the field of Women's Empowerment and Child Protection.
(3) Forms of cooperation as referred to in paragraph (1) include:
a. socialization;
b. provision of legal aid;
c. health services;
d. victim assistance;
e. counseling;
f. social rehabilitation; and
g. spiritual guidance.
(4) Procedures for implementing cooperation as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations.

CHAPTER XII
COMMUNITY PARTICIPATION
Article 51
(1) Regional Government has the obligation to strengthen Community participation in the development of Women's Empowerment and Child Protection.
(2) Community participation as referred to in paragraph (1) may be conducted in the form of:
a. socialization;
b. advocacy;
c. counseling;
d. guidance;
e. supervision;
f. facilitation;
g. services;
h. studies;
i. training;
j. capital provision; and
k. provision of facilities and infrastructure.
(3) Strengthening of Community participation as referred to in paragraph (1) may be conducted through:
a. strengthening cooperation;
b. strengthening institutional capacity for prevention and services from the Community, Islamic boarding schools and the business sector; and
c. financing support.

Article 22
(1) The Regional Government, NGOs/Social Organizations, the Community, and parents/family are obligated to provide Child Protection consisting of:
a. Children in the womb;
b. Children under five years of age;
c. School-Age Children;
d. Neglected and street children; and
e. Children who require Special Protection.
(2) The implementation of Child Protection as referred to in paragraph (1) is carried out through:
a. policies, programs, and mechanisms concerning:
1. prevention, supervision, complaints/reporting, and development of Child data information systems;
2. integrated handling for Children who are victims of violence, maltreatment, exploitation, and neglect;
3. guaranteeing the fulfillment of the rights of every Child who is a victim of crime, violence, maltreatment, exploitation, and neglect, including:
a) recovery services and health maintenance;
b) continuity of education services;
c) psychosocial services; and
d) legal aid services.
4. implementation of family support including:
a) counseling;
b) Child-rearing education;
c) family mediation; and
d) economic support.
b. increasing Community awareness and attitudes through socialization, education, and information regarding:
1. Child Rights, Child Protection, prevention of child marriage, and Child rearing; and
2. the impact of violence, maltreatment, exploitation, and Child neglect.
c. increasing the capacity of Child Protection services, which includes institutional capacity development and service provider personnel.
d. service provider personnel as referred to in letter c include:
1. health;
2. education;
3. psychosocial;
4. child rearing; and
5. legal aid.
e. increasing the ability of Children to recognize risks and dangers from situations or actions that can cause violence, exploitation, maltreatment, and neglect.
(3) The implementation of Child Protection as referred to in paragraph (2) letters a through e is carried out and/or coordinated by the SKPD responsible for Child Protection affairs.

CHAPTER XII
COMMUNITY PARTICIPATION
Article 51
(1) Regional Government has the obligation to strengthen Community participation in the development of Women's Empowerment and Child Protection.
(2) Community participation as referred to in paragraph (1) may be conducted in the form of:
a. socialization;
b. advocacy;
c. counseling;
d. guidance;
e. supervision;
f. facilitation;
g. services;
h. studies;
i. training;
j. capital provision; and
k. provision of facilities and infrastructure.
(3) Strengthening of Community participation as referred to in paragraph (1) may be conducted through:
a. strengthening cooperation;
b. strengthening institutional capacity for prevention and services from the Community, Islamic boarding schools and the business sector; and
c. financing support.

Article 52
(1) The business sector has responsibility in the implementation of Women's Empowerment and Child Protection.
(2) The form of responsibility of the business sector as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be carried out in the form of:
a. financial assistance to service institutions;
b. empowerment;
c. capacity building;
d. publication and information media;
e. provision of facilities and infrastructure;
f. establishment of special institutions for prevention and services for violence complaints; and
g. non-binding social assistance.

CHAPTER V
PROTECTION OF WOMEN
Part One General
Article 11
(1) The Regional Government is responsible for providing Protection of Women covering: efforts
a. to establish and implement policies, programs and activities for Protection of Women;
b. to undertake prevention efforts against the occurrence of Violence against Women;
c. to undertake prevention efforts against the occurrence of criminal acts of human trafficking;
d. to increase the effectiveness of services for Women and Children victims of acts of violence;
e. to increase protection efforts for Women workers;
f. to strengthen and develop institutions providing protection services for Women workers;
g. to strengthen and develop institutions providing Protection of Women services;
h. to conduct cooperation with the government, Regional Government, other Regional Governments, private parties, business sector, and/or community non-governmental organizations in efforts to prevent the occurrence of acts of Violence against Women; and
i. to involve the active role of families, religious leaders, the Community and institutions in the field of Protection of Women.
(2) The responsibility as referred to in paragraph (1), shall be implemented by Regional Apparatus that administer governmental affairs in accordance with their duties and functions.

Article 37
Special Protection for Children in emergency situations and conflict areas as referred to in Article 36 letter a shall be in the form of fulfillment of basic needs consisting of:
a. basic social services;
b. education;
c. religious guidance;
d. health services;
e. psychological counseling and/or psychotherapy;
f. legal assistance; and
g. recreational and educational activities.

Article 34
(1) Protection of School-Age Children as referred to in Article 22 paragraph (1) letter c includes:
a. receiving attention and guidance from teachers and/or supervisors without discrimination;
b. receiving attention and affection from family;
c. receiving religious guidance at school and in the Community environment;
d. receiving prevention services, health rehabilitation and care;
e. receiving compulsory education for a minimum of 9 (nine) years and being able to complete senior secondary school supported by a friendly and conducive environment;
f. receiving education guarantees;
g. receiving guidance and counseling; and
h. being provided with adequate places for play and sports.
(2) Every person and/or any party is obligated to protect School-Age Children from acts of violence, sexual harassment and food containing hazardous substances in the family, school, Office/agency/institution and Community environment.
(3) Every person and/or any party is obligated to protect School-Age Children from actions to suspend and/or expel Children from school and/or cause Children to drop out of school.

Part Two Protection of Women's Rights
Article 13
(1) The Regional Government guarantees protection of women's rights.
(2) Women's rights as referred to in paragraph (1) include the fields of:
a. education;
b. health;
c. employment;
d. politics;
e. law; and
f. economy, culture, and social.

Article 38
(1) Special Protection for Children in conflict with the law as referred to in Article 36 letter b includes Children in conflict with the law and Child victims of criminal acts, constituting the obligation and responsibility of the Regional Government, Police, Prosecutor's Office, Courts, Parents, Family and Community.
(2) The handling of Children dealing with the legal process and the implementation of Judge's decisions must continue to prioritize the Rights of the Child.
(3) Special Protection for Children in conflict with the law as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be implemented through:
a. treatment of Children in a humane manner in accordance with the dignity and Rights of the Child;
b. provision of special accompanying officers for Children from an early stage;
c. provision of special facilities and infrastructure;
d. imposition of appropriate sanctions for the best interests of the Child;
e. continuous monitoring and recording of the development of Children in conflict with the law;
f. provision of guarantees to maintain relationships with parents or family; and
g. protection from identity disclosure through mass media and to avoid labeling.
(4) In the case of a Child encountering the law, law enforcement officials shall not conduct justicia examination of the Child without the knowledge of parents or guardians, and it shall be conducted with:
a. rehabilitation efforts through special institutions for the best interests of the Child;
b. protection efforts from identity disclosure through mass media and to avoid labeling;
c. provision of safety guarantees for victim witnesses and expert witnesses, whether physical, mental or social; and
d. provision of accessibility to obtain information regarding case developments.

CHAPTER IV
WOMEN'S EMPOWERMENT
Article 7
(1) The Regional Government is responsible for Women's Empowerment.
(2) The responsibility as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be implemented by the Agency.
(3) In the implementation of Women's Empowerment, the Regional Apparatus as referred to in paragraph (1) shall involve:
a. Regional Apparatus that administers governmental affairs in the fields of:
1. population administration;
2. education;
3. Village government;
4. Regional development planning;
b. the National Commission on Women.

CHAPTER III
SCOPE
Article 6
The scope of regulation for the implementation of Women's Empowerment and Child Protection includes:
a. Women's Empowerment;
b. Women's Protection;
c. Child Protection;
d. safe houses;
e. Women's Empowerment and Child Protection by Village government;
f. family quality;
g. cooperation;
h. awards;
i. Community participation;
j. financing;
k. prohibitions;
l. investigation provisions; and
m. criminal provisions.

